<Application
    x:Class="PaulTechGuy.MQ.App.App"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <Application.Resources>
        <ResourceDictionary>
            <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
                <XamlControlsResources xmlns="using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Controls" />

                <!--
                    Marqora's chrome, on top of the accent Windows supplies.

                    Marqora used to state an accent of its own here - a teal, #51A8B1 - and
                    override every stock accent key with it. That is gone, deliberately. It
                    never fully took: a ContentDialog is hosted in the popup root rather than
                    under the window's content, and the accent-button and list-selection keys
                    are reached there through aliases the framework resolves once, so dialog
                    buttons and list selection kept the Windows accent whatever this dictionary
                    said. The app was two-toned - teal in the window, the user's own color in
                    every dialog - and the seam showed wherever the two met.

                    So the accent is the user's now, throughout, the way the title bar and Mica
                    already follow the desktop. What is left in here is the chrome that has
                    nothing to do with accent: the amber for things the user did not ask for,
                    the hairlines between the bars, and the tab strip, which is tinted FROM the
                    accent rather than replacing it.

                    What that buys, beyond consistency: the accent and the text on it are a
                    pair, and only the framework can keep the pair right for an accent it has
                    not seen. The teal needed a near-black on it, because white on #51A8B1
                    measures 1.9:1 - a pairing that was correct for that one color and could
                    not have been correct for anyone's purple.

                    This dictionary is still merged AFTER XamlControlsResources, and that still
                    matters for the keys below. A ResourceDictionary is searched in the order:
                    its own entries, then its merged dictionaries last-to-first, then its theme
                    dictionaries. Putting these in Application.Resources' ThemeDictionaries - a
                    sibling of MergedDictionaries rather than a member of it - would place them
                    behind XamlControlsResources, and every key the framework also defines
                    would resolve to the framework's value.

                    The dark dictionary is keyed "Default" rather than "Dark". Both are accepted
                    for the dark theme, but "Default" is the one the framework falls back to, so
                    keying it that way cannot leave a lookup unresolved.
                -->
                <ResourceDictionary>
                    <ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

                        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Light">
                            <!--
                                Amber, for the few things that report something the user did
                                not ask for. Marqora's own rather than SystemFillColorCaution:
                                that one moves with the OS, and this has to stay legible
                                against a known text color at 12px.

                                Dark brown on amber rather than white: 7.8:1, where white on
                                the same amber is 1.9:1 and unreadable. The same near-black is
                                used in both themes so the pair keeps its contrast when the
                                amber lightens for dark mode.
                            -->
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MqWarnBrush" Color="#FFE8A317" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MqOnWarnBrush" Color="#FF2B1A00" />

                            <!--
                                Nothing accent-colored is stated here any more. The stock
                                keys - AccentFillColorDefaultBrush, the AccentTextFill pair,
                                the TextOnAccent pair, and every ToggleButton *Checked* brush -
                                are left exactly as the framework defines them, which is in
                                terms of the user's Windows accent.

                                That is the whole point: text on the accent is the half that
                                cannot be guessed, and the framework already keeps
                                TextOnAccentFillColorPrimaryBrush correct for whatever accent
                                the user has chosen. A fixed pairing here could only be right
                                for one accent, which is what the teal was.
                            -->

                            <!--
                                The active tab.

                                The stock selected tab differs from its neighbours only by an
                                almost unpainted plate, and under Mica that difference is very
                                nearly invisible: the one thing on the strip that has to be
                                readable at a glance was the hardest thing on it to see. So
                                the tab strip is the one piece of chrome the accent reaches.

                                Brushes only - no thickness, padding or size keys.
                                UpdateTabTitles books each tab's chrome from TabView's own
                                template metrics (ClosableTabChrome in MainWindow.xaml.cs,
                                which includes the 1px border), and the strip cannot scroll,
                                so anything that changes a tab's measured width silently
                                falsifies the fit pass and clips the last tab. Color cannot
                                move a pixel - measuring the strip since confirms the border
                                is 1px in both states, so the accent edge below is free.

                                The signal is pushed from both ends: the active tab gains an
                                accent-tinted plate and an accent edge, while the inactive
                                ones sit at secondary.

                                The tints are the accent at low alpha rather than a mixed
                                color, and the text on them is left to the stock foreground.
                                A pre-mixed plate - which is what the teal #D5EBEE was - only
                                works for the one accent it was mixed from, and the selected
                                tab's text was picked to sit on that specific plate. At this
                                strength the plate barely moves the surface underneath, so the
                                ordinary text color keeps its contrast whatever the accent is,
                                and there is no second color that has to be kept in step.

                                ThemeResource rather than a StaticResource alias, deliberately:
                                an alias resolves once, which is the trap described above for
                                TabViewBorderBrush and again in docs/BROKEN_TAB_BAR.md. The
                                shade is the one the framework's own accent fill uses in this
                                theme, so the strip agrees with the accent buttons exactly.
                            -->
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewItemHeaderBackgroundSelected"
                                             Color="{ThemeResource SystemAccentColorDark1}"
                                             Opacity="0.18" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewSelectedItemBorderBrush"
                                             Color="{ThemeResource SystemAccentColorDark1}" />

                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewItemHeaderForeground" Color="#9E000000" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewItemHeaderCloseButtonForeground" Color="#9E000000" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewItemHeaderForegroundPointerOver" Color="#E4000000" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewItemHeaderBackgroundPointerOver"
                                             Color="{ThemeResource SystemAccentColorDark1}"
                                             Opacity="0.10" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewItemHeaderBackgroundPressed"
                                             Color="{ThemeResource SystemAccentColorDark1}"
                                             Opacity="0.18" />

                            <!--
                                The chrome rules: the hairline under the tab strip, under the
                                menu bar and under the format bar, the one over the status bar,
                                and the short vertical bars that group the format toolbar.

                                Stock, these are CardStrokeColorDefault and
                                DividerStrokeColorDefault - both 6% black in this theme, and in
                                dark CardStrokeColorDefault is 10% *black*, which is a shadow
                                edge meant to sit under a raised card rather than a divider
                                drawn on a flat surface. Neither has much to work against on an
                                unpainted row: the chrome here is left to Mica, so the backdrop
                                is translucent and takes its tint from the desktop, and whatever
                                contrast the line had varied with the wallpaper behind the
                                window.

                                Marqora's own keys rather than an override of the stock ones,
                                because CardStrokeColorDefaultBrush is also on the empty-state
                                drop target, where a barely-there card edge is exactly right.

                                TabViewBorderBrush has to be restated even though stock aliases
                                it to CardStrokeColorDefault. That alias is a StaticResource,
                                resolved once inside the framework dictionary, so rewriting the
                                color here would never reach the line TabView draws along the
                                foot of the strip - and that line sits a few pixels above the
                                menu bar's own, where any mismatch between the two is obvious.
                            -->
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MqChromeRuleBrush" Color="#36000000" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MqChromeDividerBrush" Color="#32000000" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewBorderBrush" Color="#36000000" />
                        </ResourceDictionary>

                        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Default">
                            <!-- Lifted a little for dark mode. 9.1:1. -->
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MqWarnBrush" Color="#FFF2B434" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MqOnWarnBrush" Color="#FF2B1A00" />

                            <!--
                                As in the Light dictionary: every accent-colored stock key is
                                left to the framework, which states it in terms of the user's
                                Windows accent.
                            -->

                            <!--
                                The active tab - see the Light dictionary for why the strip is
                                painted at all and why this is brushes only.

                                A heavier tint than the light theme's, for the reason the deep
                                teal plate had: the same alpha that lifts off a white page is
                                lost against a dark strip. SystemAccentColorLight2 is the shade
                                the framework's own accent fill uses in dark, where the raw
                                accent is often too dark to read against the background.
                            -->
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewItemHeaderBackgroundSelected"
                                             Color="{ThemeResource SystemAccentColorLight2}"
                                             Opacity="0.26" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewSelectedItemBorderBrush"
                                             Color="{ThemeResource SystemAccentColorLight2}" />

                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewItemHeaderForeground" Color="#C5FFFFFF" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewItemHeaderCloseButtonForeground" Color="#C5FFFFFF" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewItemHeaderForegroundPointerOver" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewItemHeaderBackgroundPointerOver"
                                             Color="{ThemeResource SystemAccentColorLight2}"
                                             Opacity="0.14" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewItemHeaderBackgroundPressed"
                                             Color="{ThemeResource SystemAccentColorLight2}"
                                             Opacity="0.24" />

                            <!--
                                The chrome rules - see the Light dictionary for what these are
                                and why they are Marqora's own keys. White rather than black
                                here: the stock rule is 10% black on a dark row, which is the
                                whole of why the dividers were hardest to find in this theme.
                            -->
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MqChromeRuleBrush" Color="#30FFFFFF" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MqChromeDividerBrush" Color="#2CFFFFFF" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabViewBorderBrush" Color="#30FFFFFF" />
                        </ResourceDictionary>

                    </ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>
                </ResourceDictionary>

                <!--
                    Every menu in the app - the header bar, both context menus and the ones
                    the diagram and cheatsheet windows build in code - takes its font,
                    spacing and colors from this one dictionary. Merged last for the same
                    reason as the accent above: a dictionary merged after
                    XamlControlsResources wins, one merged before it does not.
                -->
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Menus.xaml" />
            </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>

            <!-- Shared metrics, so spacing stays consistent across the chrome. -->
            <x:Double x:Key="MqToolbarHeight">48</x:Double>
            <!--
                Shorter than the chrome row above it. The buttons are 34 tall either way,
                and four stacked strips over a 420-pixel minimum window height is already
                most of what a small window has to give.
            -->
            <x:Double x:Key="MqFormatBarHeight">40</x:Double>

            <!--
                Tabs take their natural width up to this cap; a longer name is shortened
                from the middle to fit it rather than widening the tab. Short names stay
                short, long ones all stop at the same place.
            -->
            <x:Double x:Key="TabViewItemMaxWidth">220</x:Double>

            <!--
                The tab's tooltip is the only place the full path is shown, and WinUI's stock
                320 wrapped most of them onto a second line mid-folder. Widening the cap by
                30% keeps a typical path on one line without letting a deep one run the width
                of the screen. The key is the framework's own, so every tooltip in the app
                takes it - which is the point: the tab list menu and the recent files list
                show the same paths.
            -->
            <x:Double x:Key="ToolTipContentMaxWidth">416</x:Double>

            <x:Double x:Key="MqTitleBarHeight">44</x:Double>
            <x:Double x:Key="MqTabStripHeight">40</x:Double>
            <Thickness x:Key="MqToolbarPadding">10,0,10,0</Thickness>
            <CornerRadius x:Key="MqPillRadius">6</CornerRadius>

            <!--
                The three control heights, stated once each. See docs/Button-App-Standards.md
                for the argument; the short form is that they are not interchangeable.

                34 is the main window's chrome rows, sized to the 48-tall toolbar they sit in.
                It is Marqora's own number and applies to nothing else.

                32 is a form row - a text box, a drop-down, the button beside them - and the
                floor under every command button. It is not chosen: it is what the framework's
                own Button, TextBox and ComboBox come out at with their stock padding, so a
                row built from stock controls is already 32 and a footer that says 32 is
                agreeing with the platform rather than overriding it.

                28 is a palette window's own toolbar. Those windows go down to 320x240, and at
                34 the strip would take a fifth of the height the content needs.
            -->
            <x:Double x:Key="MqChromeButtonHeight">34</x:Double>
            <x:Double x:Key="MqFormRowHeight">32</x:Double>
            <x:Double x:Key="MqCompactButtonHeight">28</x:Double>

            <!--
                The width floor under a command button. The one thing the framework does not
                supply: without it a "No" sits at 40 pixels beside a "Discard changes" at 140
                and the pair reads as two unrelated controls rather than as one question.
            -->
            <x:Double x:Key="MqCommandButtonMinWidth">96</x:Double>

            <!--
                Between buttons in a row. The one metric the app was already consistent about,
                and the same 8 the framework spaces a ContentDialog's own footer with.
            -->
            <x:Double x:Key="MqButtonGroupSpacing">8</x:Double>

            <!--
                A quiet toolbar button: no chrome until the pointer arrives. Keeps the
                command bar visually calm while remaining a full-size hit target.
            -->
            <Style x:Key="MqToolButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
                <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,6" />
                <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="36" />
                <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource MqChromeButtonHeight}" />
                <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource MqPillRadius}" />
                <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
                <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13" />
            </Style>

            <!--
                Square, glyph only. Derived rather than restated so it cannot drift from the
                tool button it sits beside; BasedOn looks backwards only, hence the order.
            -->
            <Style x:Key="MqIconButtonStyle" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource MqToolButtonStyle}">
                <Setter Property="Width" Value="{StaticResource MqChromeButtonHeight}" />
                <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="{StaticResource MqChromeButtonHeight}" />
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
            </Style>

            <!--
                A palette window's toolbar. Same shape, one step denser, because these windows
                go down to 320x240 and the strip is competing with the content for the height.
                webshell/diagram.css carries the same numbers for the diagram window's HTML
                toolbar; build/Test-ButtonStandards.ps1 is what keeps the two in step.
            -->
            <Style x:Key="MqCompactToolButtonStyle" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource MqToolButtonStyle}">
                <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource MqCompactButtonHeight}" />
                <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="32" />
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,0" />
            </Style>

            <!--
                The button a dialog or a window ends with: OK, Cancel, Export, Find All.

                Nothing here but size. The chrome - padding, radius, font, border, and every
                hover and pressed color - is the framework's, which is the point: a command
                button should look like a Windows command button and not like Marqora's idea
                of one.
            -->
            <Style x:Key="MqCommandButtonStyle" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultButtonStyle}">
                <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="{StaticResource MqCommandButtonMinWidth}" />
                <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{StaticResource MqFormRowHeight}" />
                <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
            </Style>

            <!--
                The one button on a surface that commits, in the user's Windows accent.

                Padding, font and border thickness are restated rather than inherited, and
                that is not belt and braces: AccentButtonStyle in the Windows App SDK declares
                no BasedOn and sets six properties - Foreground, Background, BackgroundSizing,
                BorderBrush, CornerRadius and Template. Deriving from it and assuming the rest
                arrives is how an accent button ends up a different size from the neutral one
                beside it.
            -->
            <Style x:Key="MqPrimaryCommandButtonStyle" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource AccentButtonStyle}">
                <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="{StaticResource MqCommandButtonMinWidth}" />
                <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{StaticResource MqFormRowHeight}" />
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource ButtonPadding}" />
                <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{ThemeResource ContentControlThemeFontFamily}" />
                <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{ThemeResource ControlContentThemeFontSize}" />
                <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{ThemeResource ButtonBorderThemeThickness}" />
                <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
                <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
            </Style>

            <!-- A split action standing in an action row: Reload All, on the change notice. -->
            <Style x:Key="MqCommandDropDownStyle" TargetType="DropDownButton">
                <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="{StaticResource MqCommandButtonMinWidth}" />
                <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{StaticResource MqFormRowHeight}" />
                <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
            </Style>

            <!--
                The empty state's four entry actions.

                Roomier than a footer button because they are the page rather than the foot of
                it: with no document open they are the only things on screen to press. The
                padding and margin are what the markup carried inline, moved here so they are
                said once rather than four times.

                They are deliberately NOT command buttons. The row of four measures about 570
                and folds to two rows below EmptyStateStackWidth - 700, in MainWindow.xaml.cs -
                and narrowing these to the 96 floor would make that threshold fold a row that
                still fitted. The margin stays a margin rather than becoming ColumnSpacing for
                the reason given at the Grid itself.
            -->
            <Style x:Key="MqPageActionButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="20,8" />
                <Setter Property="Margin" Value="5,0" />
                <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
                <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
            </Style>

            <Style x:Key="MqPageActionPrimaryStyle" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource AccentButtonStyle}">
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="20,8" />
                <Setter Property="Margin" Value="5,0" />
                <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{ThemeResource ContentControlThemeFontFamily}" />
                <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{ThemeResource ControlContentThemeFontSize}" />
                <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{ThemeResource ButtonBorderThemeThickness}" />
                <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
                <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
            </Style>

            <!--
                A recent-file card, and the two square controls that sit beside it.

                38 rather than the 34 an icon button wears elsewhere. That is the one size in
                the app the standard has not resolved: these sit in a two-line card rather than
                a control row, so the taller square may well be right - but it is a judgement
                that wants eyes on it, and until then the number is at least stated once here
                instead of six times in the markup.
            -->
            <Style x:Key="MqCardButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="14,10" />
                <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="8" />
                <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
                <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
            </Style>

            <Style x:Key="MqCardIconButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
                <Setter Property="Width" Value="38" />
                <Setter Property="Height" Value="38" />
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
                <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="8" />
                <Setter Property="Margin" Value="4,0,0,0" />
            </Style>

            <Style x:Key="MqCardIconToggleStyle" TargetType="ToggleButton">
                <Setter Property="Width" Value="38" />
                <Setter Property="Height" Value="38" />
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
                <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="8" />
                <Setter Property="Margin" Value="4,0,0,0" />
            </Style>

            <Style x:Key="MqToolToggleStyle" TargetType="ToggleButton">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
                <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,6" />
                <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="36" />
                <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource MqChromeButtonHeight}" />
                <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource MqPillRadius}" />
                <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
                <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13" />
            </Style>

            <!--
                The folder line under a recent file's name. It reads as the plain caption it
                has always been and only grows a hit target once the pointer is on it, so the
                card stays quiet while the path stays clickable.
            -->
            <Style x:Key="MqPathLinkStyle" TargetType="Button">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
                <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="5,1" />
                <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
                <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
                <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="4" />
                <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left" />
                <!-- Cancels the padding, so the path keeps its left edge under the file name. -->
                <Setter Property="Margin" Value="-5,0,0,0" />
            </Style>

            <Style x:Key="MqDropDownStyle" TargetType="DropDownButton">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
                <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,6" />
                <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource MqChromeButtonHeight}" />
                <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource MqPillRadius}" />
                <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
                <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13" />
            </Style>

            <!--
                Segmented view switcher: the three buttons read as one control.

                This carries its own template rather than restyling the stock one, and the
                reason is text quality. The default ToggleButton animates on press and on
                check, and while an animation runs the control's content is composited into
                its own layer - which drops the label from ClearType to grayscale
                antialiasing. The text visibly softens and only sharpens again once the
                layer is discarded, which on these three buttons was the whole of the
                blurry-label problem. The template below changes state by swapping brushes,
                so no layer is ever created and the labels stay sharp.
            -->
            <Style x:Key="MqSegmentStyle" TargetType="ToggleButton">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
                <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
                <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="5" />
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="14,5" />
                <Setter Property="Height" Value="30" />
                <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
                <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12.5" />
                <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
                <Setter Property="Template">
                    <Setter.Value>
                        <ControlTemplate TargetType="ToggleButton">
                            <Grid x:Name="Root"
                                  Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                  CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}"
                                  Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">

                                <VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                                    <VisualStateGroup x:Name="CommonStates">
                                        <VisualState x:Name="Normal" />

                                        <VisualState x:Name="PointerOver">
                                            <VisualState.Setters>
                                                <Setter Target="Root.Background"
                                                        Value="{ThemeResource SubtleFillColorSecondaryBrush}" />
                                            </VisualState.Setters>
                                        </VisualState>

                                        <VisualState x:Name="Pressed">
                                            <VisualState.Setters>
                                                <Setter Target="Root.Background"
                                                        Value="{ThemeResource SubtleFillColorTertiaryBrush}" />
                                            </VisualState.Setters>
                                        </VisualState>

                                        <VisualState x:Name="Disabled">
                                            <VisualState.Setters>
                                                <Setter Target="Label.Foreground"
                                                        Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorDisabledBrush}" />
                                            </VisualState.Setters>
                                        </VisualState>

                                        <VisualState x:Name="Checked">
                                            <VisualState.Setters>
                                                <Setter Target="Root.Background"
                                                        Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}" />
                                                <Setter Target="Label.Foreground"
                                                        Value="{ThemeResource TextOnAccentFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
                                            </VisualState.Setters>
                                        </VisualState>

                                        <VisualState x:Name="CheckedPointerOver">
                                            <VisualState.Setters>
                                                <Setter Target="Root.Background"
                                                        Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}" />
                                                <Setter Target="Label.Foreground"
                                                        Value="{ThemeResource TextOnAccentFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
                                            </VisualState.Setters>
                                        </VisualState>

                                        <VisualState x:Name="CheckedPressed">
                                            <VisualState.Setters>
                                                <Setter Target="Root.Background"
                                                        Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}" />
                                                <Setter Target="Label.Foreground"
                                                        Value="{ThemeResource TextOnAccentFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
                                            </VisualState.Setters>
                                        </VisualState>

                                        <VisualState x:Name="CheckedDisabled">
                                            <VisualState.Setters>
                                                <Setter Target="Root.Background"
                                                        Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDisabledBrush}" />
                                            </VisualState.Setters>
                                        </VisualState>
                                    </VisualStateGroup>
                                </VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>

                                <ContentPresenter x:Name="Label"
                                                  Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                                                  Foreground="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                                                  FontSize="{TemplateBinding FontSize}"
                                                  HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                                  VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                            </Grid>
                        </ControlTemplate>
                    </Setter.Value>
                </Setter>
            </Style>

            <Style x:Key="MqIconStyle" TargetType="FontIcon">
                <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="15" />
                <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{ThemeResource SymbolThemeFontFamily}" />
            </Style>

            <Style x:Key="MqCaptionTextStyle" TargetType="TextBlock">
                <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}" />
                <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
            </Style>

        </ResourceDictionary>
    </Application.Resources>

</Application>
